WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.500
What does TIC 3.0 actually require?

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.618
The book reads it from
the current documents

3
00:00:04.618 --> 00:00:06.450
rather than from what's said about them.

4
00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:10.450
CISA republished the whole
set in September 2026.

5
00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:15.947
The revision tables say the only 2026
changes were updated footnote links,

6
00:00:15.947 --> 00:00:21.888
so the substance dates from July 2025, and
April 2021 for the Traditional use case.

7
00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:25.514
One practical note for
anyone writing a package:

8
00:00:25.514 --> 00:00:30.500
CISA's landing page labels three
volumes 1.1, but the covers read 1.2.

9
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:31.562
Cite the cover.

10
00:00:31.562 --> 00:00:36.775
There are four use cases: Traditional,
Branch Office, Remote User and Cloud.

11
00:00:36.775 --> 00:00:38.737
There is no SASE use case.

12
00:00:38.737 --> 00:00:42.023
An agency deploying SASE
at its branches operates

13
00:00:42.023 --> 00:00:44.774
under the Branch Office
and Cloud use cases.

14
00:00:44.774 --> 00:00:48.668
The program guidebook
calls TIC 3.0 descriptive,

15
00:00:48.668 --> 00:00:52.713
not prescriptive, and the catalog
says it sets goals, not methods.

16
00:00:52.713 --> 00:00:55.062
But two statements constrain a branch.

17
00:00:55.062 --> 00:00:59.453
Policy enforcement parity: once
you decide a capability is needed,

18
00:00:59.453 --> 00:01:02.567
you apply it on every
path, and the Branch Office

19
00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:06.000
use case names SD-WAN paths
as one place that applies.

20
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.713
And preserve CISA's visibility as branches
connect directly to external services.

21
00:01:11.713 --> 00:01:17.329
Discretion also carries an obligation: where
TIC controls don't cover residual risk,

22
00:01:17.329 --> 00:01:21.100
agencies must apply controls
or compensating controls.

23
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:26.175
CISA's June 2026 SASE guide
states the consequence plainly.

24
00:01:26.175 --> 00:01:31.406
TIC 3.0 doesn't require routing
traffic through TIC access points if

25
00:01:31.406 --> 00:01:36.013
an alternative gives both the agency
and CISA situational awareness.

26
00:01:36.013 --> 00:01:38.513
That guide permits and describes SASE.

27
00:01:38.513 --> 00:01:39.938
It doesn't require it.

28
00:01:39.938 --> 00:01:42.830
So when someone says TIC requires SD-WAN,

29
00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:47.300
or that TIC forbids local breakout,
both claims fail the check.

30
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.579
The honest position is narrower
and stronger: TIC sets goals,

31
00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:55.174
local breakout is allowed,
and the agency must show

32
00:01:55.174 --> 00:01:59.090
parity and preserve CISA's
view on every path it opens.
